Warning:

20
00:01:35.747 --> 00:01:37.155
Sources and honesty.

21
00:01:37.435 --> 00:01:44.625
Indian Railways' reservation system (PRS) is built and run by CRIS, and ticketing on the web is through IRCTC.

22
00:01:44.905 --> 00:01:47.550
Their internal designs are not fully public.

23
00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:59.521
Rules such as quota types, Tatkal opening times, advance reservation period, charting times, refund rules and ID requirements change over time and are summarised with VERIFY.

24
00:01:59.801 --> 00:02:07.267
GDS, NDC and airline rules are industry standards and practices, also marked VERIFY where specific.

25
00:02:07.547 --> 00:02:09.083
This is a reference design.

26
00:02:10.433 --> 00:02:11.287
Part 1.

27
00:02:11.567 --> 00:02:13.103
The System in One Page.

28
00:02:14.353 --> 00:02:16.038
Why train ticketing is special.

29
00:02:16.318 --> 00:02:17.747
A flight is one hop:

30
00:02:18.027 --> 00:02:19.947
180 seats from A to B.

31
00:02:20.227 --> 00:02:23.641
A long-distance train is one vehicle serving many journeys.

32
00:02:23.921 --> 00:02:32.347
A berth from Delhi to Mumbai via 20 stations can be sold to one passenger for Delhi, then Kota and another for Kota, then Mumbai.

33
00:02:32.627 --> 00:02:53.363
The system must: track each berth's occupancy per segment of the route;. apply quotas: general, Tatkal, ladies, senior citizen, and pooled quotas for intermediate stations (VERIFY current quota names and rules);. sell waitlisted tickets that may confirm later when others cancel;. handle RAC (a shared berth, with a seat guaranteed);.

34
00:02:54.713 --> 00:03:02.415
produce a final chart before departure;. survive demand spikes where far more people want seats than exist, all within minutes.

35
00:03:03.765 --> 00:03:04.853
Main systems.

36
00:03:05.133 --> 00:03:06.477
Here is a quick table:

37
00:03:06.757 --> 00:03:08.271
System, and What it does.

38
00:03:08.551 --> 00:03:10.109
Train master and schedules:

39
00:03:10.389 --> 00:03:14.058
Trains, routes, stops, coach compositions, running days, fares.

40
00:03:14.338 --> 00:03:15.618
Inventory service:

41
00:03:15.898 --> 00:03:20.229
Seat or berth availability per train, date, class, quota and segment.

42
00:03:20.509 --> 00:03:21.554
Booking engine:

43
00:03:21.834 --> 00:03:26.207
Allocates berths or waitlist positions atomically; creates the PNR.

44
00:03:26.487 --> 00:03:28.493
Waitlist and RAC manager:

45
00:03:28.773 --> 00:03:32.677
Promotes waitlisted passengers on cancellations; final charting.

46
00:03:32.957 --> 00:03:34.365
Availability service:

47
00:03:34.645 --> 00:03:38.463
Fast, cached answers to how many seats? across trains and dates.

48
00:03:38.743 --> 00:03:39.746
Fare engine:

49
00:03:40.026 --> 00:03:45.231
Base fares by class and distance, dynamic fares where applicable, charges, taxes.

50
00:03:45.511 --> 00:03:46.365
Payments:

51
00:03:46.645 --> 00:03:50.421
Gateways, UPI, cards, wallets; reconciliation; refunds.

52
00:03:50.701 --> 00:03:51.746
User accounts:

53
00:03:52.026 --> 00:03:56.271
Registration, verification, saved passengers, agent accounts.

54
00:03:57.621 --> 00:03:59.115
Admission and anti-bot:

55
00:03:59.395 --> 00:04:03.341
Waiting room, CAPTCHA, rate limits, bot detection at Tatkal time.

56
00:04:03.621 --> 00:04:05.179
Cancellation and refunds:

57
00:04:05.459 --> 00:04:08.509
Rules by time before departure and ticket status.

58
00:04:08.789 --> 00:04:09.899
Notifications:

59
00:04:10.179 --> 00:04:13.571
PNR status updates, chart prepared, platform info.

60
00:04:13.851 --> 00:04:15.515
OTA flights and buses:

61
00:04:15.795 --> 00:04:22.216
Search, fare caching, supplier connections (GDS, NDC, bus operators), booking, ticketing.

62
00:04:22.496 --> 00:04:23.691
Data platform:

63
00:04:23.971 --> 00:04:27.021
Demand analytics, forecasting, fraud, reporting.

64
00:04:28.371 --> 00:04:32.169
Scale for this design (assumptions; VERIFY real figures).

65
00:04:32.449 --> 00:04:33.793
Here is a quick table:

66
00:04:34.073 --> 00:04:35.395
Quantity, and Value.

67
00:04:35.675 --> 00:04:39.793
Trains per day: ~13,000 passenger trains (VERIFY).

68
00:04:40.073 --> 00:04:45.897
Reserved tickets per day: ~1.5M (web + counters + apps; VERIFY).

69
00:04:46.177 --> 00:04:47.414
Registered users:

70
00:04:47.694 --> 00:04:48.931
Hundreds of millions.

71
00:04:49.211 --> 00:04:50.278
Tatkal window:

72
00:04:50.558 --> 00:04:56.745
Opens daily at a fixed time for AC classes and a later time for non-AC (VERIFY times).

73
00:04:57.025 --> 00:04:58.326
Peak booking attempts:

74
00:04:58.606 --> 00:05:04.131
Tens of thousands per second in the first minute; far more availability checks and page loads.

75
00:05:04.411 --> 00:05:06.182
Availability queries per day:

76
00:05:06.462 --> 00:05:09.577
Hundreds of millions (users compare trains and dates).

77
00:05:10.927 --> 00:05:12.825
Estimation, the Tatkal minute:

78
00:05:13.105 --> 00:05:23.623
Suppose 200k Tatkal berths go on sale at opening, and 2M users are ready to book, each checking availability several times and submitting once or twice.

79
00:05:23.903 --> 00:05:32.607
In the first 2 minutes, the booking engine faces ~20k booking submissions per second plus 100k+ availability checks per second.

80
00:05:32.887 --> 00:05:35.809
Most berths on popular trains sell out in seconds.

81
00:05:37.159 --> 00:05:42.087
The design goal isn't to satisfy everyone, because demand is 10 times supply.

82
00:05:42.367 --> 00:05:48.319
It is to stay up, be fair (first valid request wins), never double-sell, and tell losers quickly.

83
00:05:49.669 --> 00:05:50.544
Part 2.

84
00:05:50.824 --> 00:05:51.784
Requirements.

85
00:05:53.034 --> 00:05:54.442
Functional (railways).

86
00:05:54.722 --> 00:05:59.330
Search trains between stations for a date, with availability per class and quota.

87
00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:09.722
Book up to a maximum number of passengers per ticket (VERIFY limits), with berth preferences, senior citizen and child rules, and ID details where required.

88
00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:13.693
Allocate confirmed berths, RAC or waitlist positions.

89
00:06:13.973 --> 00:06:19.455
Tatkal booking with its own quota, opening time, charges and refund rules (VERIFY).

90
00:06:19.735 --> 00:06:22.893
Cancel, fully or partially, with refunds by rules.

91
00:06:24.243 --> 00:06:26.973
Waitlist promotion as cancellations happen.

92
00:06:27.253 --> 00:06:33.632
Final chart before departure, with a second chart for last-minute vacancies (VERIFY charting times).

93
00:06:33.912 --> 00:06:36.749
PNR status enquiries; notifications.

94
00:06:37.029 --> 00:06:42.256
Agents with separate limits and timing restrictions (VERIFY agent rules during Tatkal).

95
00:06:43.606 --> 00:06:45.099
Functional (OTA).

96
00:06:45.379 --> 00:06:49.219
Search flights (one-way, return, multi-city) across airlines.

97
00:06:49.499 --> 00:06:52.145
Show fares, fare families and baggage rules.

98
00:06:52.425 --> 00:06:54.729
Price-check and book, then issue the ticket.

99
00:06:55.009 --> 00:06:57.739
Handle schedule changes and cancellations.

100
00:06:58.019 --> 00:07:01.155
Search and book buses from many operators with seat maps.

101
00:07:02.505 --> 00:07:03.615
Non-functional.

102
00:07:03.895 --> 00:07:05.239
Here is a quick table:

103
00:07:05.519 --> 00:07:06.884
Attribute, and Target.

104
00:07:07.164 --> 00:07:08.081
Correctness:

105
00:07:08.361 --> 00:07:14.420
Never allocate the same berth-segment twice; waitlist order strictly by booking time per quota rules.

106
00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:15.575
Fairness:

107
00:07:15.855 --> 00:07:20.825
First-come first-served among valid requests; bots and scripted agents excluded.

108
00:07:21.105 --> 00:07:22.172
Availability:

109
00:07:22.452 --> 00:07:25.887
Stay up through the Tatkal minute; degrade search before booking.

110
00:07:26.167 --> 00:07:27.084
Latency:

111
00:07:27.364 --> 00:07:31.161
Booking confirmation (allocation) in seconds; search in 1 s.

112
00:07:31.441 --> 00:07:32.295
Payments:

113
00:07:32.575 --> 00:07:37.140
No ticket without payment; no payment without a ticket or a timely refund.

114
00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:38.231
Audit:

115
00:07:38.511 --> 00:07:41.732
Every booking, cancellation and chart change recorded.

116
00:07:43.082 --> 00:07:43.978
Part 3.

117
00:07:44.258 --> 00:07:45.773
High-Level Architecture.

118
00:07:47.023 --> 00:07:48.367
Here is the diagram.

119
00:07:52.217 --> 00:07:53.283
Key decisions:

120
00:07:54.633 --> 00:07:55.977
Here is a quick table:

121
00:07:56.257 --> 00:07:58.028
Decision, and Choice, and Why.

122
00:07:58.308 --> 00:07:59.225
Partitioning.

123
00:07:59.505 --> 00:08:00.337
Choice:

124
00:08:00.617 --> 00:08:06.740
Inventory and booking partitioned by (train, journey date); each partition owned by one booking worker.

125
00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:07.703
Why:

126
00:08:07.983 --> 00:08:16.004
All bookings for one train-date must be serialised to avoid double allocation; different trains are independent (Ch 11).

127
00:08:16.284 --> 00:08:17.948
Inventory representation.

128
00:08:18.228 --> 00:08:19.060
Choice:

129
00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:23.500
Per berth, a bitset over route segments; per quota, pools of berths.

130
00:08:23.780 --> 00:08:24.463
Why:

131
00:08:24.743 --> 00:08:28.412
Fast overlap checks and allocation (§6.1).

132
00:08:28.692 --> 00:08:29.759
Availability.

133
00:08:30.039 --> 00:08:30.871
Choice:

134
00:08:31.151 --> 00:08:36.655
A cache updated from inventory changes; search never reads the booking engine directly.

135
00:08:36.935 --> 00:08:37.617
Why:

136
00:08:37.897 --> 00:08:39.284
Protect the booking path.

137
00:08:39.564 --> 00:08:41.441
Payment and allocation order.

138
00:08:41.721 --> 00:08:42.553
Choice:

139
00:08:42.833 --> 00:08:48.487
Allocate (hold) first, then pay, confirm on payment success; release on failure or timeout.

140
00:08:48.767 --> 00:08:49.449
Why:

141
00:08:49.729 --> 00:08:53.847
Avoid taking money for nothing; bounded holds (Ch 46).

142
00:08:55.197 --> 00:08:56.306
Tatkal defence.

143
00:08:56.586 --> 00:08:57.418
Choice:

144
00:08:57.698 --> 00:09:02.882
Waiting room + bot defence + per-user limits + fair queueing into partitions.

145
00:09:03.162 --> 00:09:03.845
Why:

146
00:09:04.125 --> 00:09:05.234
Demand ≫ supply.

147
00:09:06.584 --> 00:09:07.501
Part 4.

148
00:09:07.781 --> 00:09:09.659
How It Works, Journeys End to End.

149
00:09:10.909 --> 00:09:12.935
Journey 1: searching for a train.

150
00:09:13.215 --> 00:09:17.418
Search: the user enters New Delhi, then Mumbai Central, 15 Nov.

151
00:09:17.698 --> 00:09:26.082
The train search service finds trains serving both stations in the right order on that day, from the timetable graph (§6.6).

152
00:09:26.362 --> 00:09:30.458
Results are cached per (from, to, date), because schedules change rarely.

153
00:09:30.738 --> 00:09:35.751
Availability per class and quota comes from the availability cache, not the booking engine.

154
00:09:36.031 --> 00:09:40.511
Each entry is the count of berths free for that segment, or the waitlist number:

155
00:09:40.791 --> 00:09:45.101
AVAILABLE-0042, RAC 12, WL 35.

156
00:09:45.381 --> 00:09:46.255
Freshness:

157
00:09:47.605 --> 00:09:58.080
the cache is updated from inventory change events within seconds;. in rush periods, entries may be marked availability may change quickly;. the booking engine is the truth.

158
00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:10.328
Fare: the fare engine computes the fare per class: base fare by distance slab and class, reservation charge, superfast charge, GST for AC classes (VERIFY components).

159
00:10:10.608 --> 00:10:14.896
Dynamic fares apply on some premium trains (§6.7).

160
00:10:16.246 --> 00:10:18.123
Journey 2: a regular booking.

161
00:10:18.403 --> 00:10:26.617
Submit: the user selects a train and class, adds passengers (name, age, gender, berth preference, ID where needed) and submits.

162
00:10:26.897 --> 00:10:36.795
Admission: the gateway checks the session, the per-user booking limits (tickets per month per verified user; VERIFY limits) and CAPTCHA if the risk score is high.

163
00:10:37.075 --> 00:10:40.403
The request is routed to the booking worker owning (train, date).

164
00:10:40.683 --> 00:10:53.206
Allocation (§6.1, §6.2), inside the worker, serially for that train-date: find berths in the right quota pool free for every segment from boarding to destination;.

165
00:10:54.556 --> 00:11:15.164
honour preferences where possible (lower berths for seniors and women with children per rules; VERIFY);. keep the group together if possible;. if not enough confirmed berths remain: allocate RAC (sharing a side-lower berth) if available, else a waitlist number in the quota's waitlist;. hold the allocation for a short payment window.

166
00:11:15.444 --> 00:11:20.372
Payment: the user pays via the gateway (UPI, cards, net banking, wallets).

167
00:11:21.722 --> 00:11:24.239
On success, the allocation becomes booked.

168
00:11:24.519 --> 00:11:31.453
A PNR is created with passengers, berths or status, fare and payment reference, and the e-ticket is sent.

169
00:11:31.733 --> 00:11:36.362
On failure or timeout, the hold is released and the availability cache updated.

170
00:11:36.642 --> 00:11:48.567
Unclear payment status: if the gateway reports success after the hold expired (late callback), the system must either allocate if still possible or auto-refund within a defined time.

171
00:11:48.847 --> 00:11:53.541
It must never keep money without a ticket (Ch 45, I03).

172
00:11:54.891 --> 00:11:56.704
Journey 3: the Tatkal minute.

173
00:11:56.984 --> 00:12:04.131
It is 09:59:30 and 2 million users are logged in with passengers pre-filled (master list).

174
00:12:04.411 --> 00:12:24.677
Before opening: static pages and scripts are served from the CDN;. users are admitted to a waiting room at login or near opening, so login and session creation don't all happen at 10:00;. a server-synchronised countdown (the same idea as I02 L1) prevents early submissions.

175
00:12:26.027 --> 00:12:44.971
At opening: submissions go through anti-bot checks (§6.5) and per-user and per-IP limits;. the admission controller passes requests to booking partitions at the rate each partition can process;. requests for the same train-date queue in arrival order (FIFO) at that partition.

176
00:12:45.251 --> 00:12:52.078
Allocation: each partition's worker processes its queue serially: allocate or waitlist, hold, return.

177
00:12:53.428 --> 00:12:55.455
Most popular trains fill in seconds.

178
00:12:55.735 --> 00:13:03.201
Later requests get waitlist numbers, or not available once the waitlist quota is exhausted (VERIFY waitlist limits).

179
00:13:03.481 --> 00:13:06.916
Payment peak: thousands of payments per second hit gateways.

180
00:13:07.196 --> 00:13:13.319
Payment orchestration spreads the load across gateways, with health checks (Ch 45 L4).

181
00:13:13.599 --> 00:13:17.375
Holds are long enough to complete payment under peak load (a few minutes).

182
00:13:18.725 --> 00:13:25.935
Expired holds go back into the pool, and are re-offered to queued requests in order, not to whoever refreshes fastest.

183
00:13:26.215 --> 00:13:33.362
Communication: users see their queue position and a quick answer (waitlisted WL 42, not available).

184
00:13:33.642 --> 00:13:36.543
The availability cache is updated continuously.

185
00:13:36.823 --> 00:13:41.218
The search tier may be degraded (cached results only) to protect booking.

186
00:13:41.498 --> 00:13:44.399
Aftermath: the booking data feeds fraud analysis.

187
00:13:44.679 --> 00:13:48.946
Accounts with bot-like patterns are flagged and cancelled per rules (VERIFY).

188
00:13:50.296 --> 00:13:53.325
Journey 4: a cancellation and the waitlist moves.

189
00:13:53.605 --> 00:13:58.085
Cancellation: a confirmed passenger cancels one day before departure.

190
00:13:58.365 --> 00:14:07.475
The refund is computed by rules (flat charges by class, percentage deductions by time before departure, special Tatkal rules; VERIFY).

191
00:14:07.755 --> 00:14:12.256
Release: the inventory worker for that train-date releases the berth-segment.

192
00:14:12.536 --> 00:14:24.632
Promotion: the waitlist manager promotes in order: the first RAC passenger(s) move to the confirmed berth (fitting segments);. the first waitlisted passenger moves to RAC.

193
00:14:25.982 --> 00:14:30.675
This is per quota, with pooled-quota rules for intermediate stations (VERIFY).

194
00:14:30.955 --> 00:14:37.889
Notification: promoted passengers are notified by SMS or app, and the PNR status updates.

195
00:14:38.169 --> 00:14:39.043
Charting:

196
00:14:39.323 --> 00:14:51.398
First chart: a set time before departure, the final allocation is frozen and waitlisted passengers who didn't confirm are cancelled with an automatic refund (for e-tickets; VERIFY).

197
00:14:51.678 --> 00:14:58.483
Second chart: remaining vacancies are released for current booking until close to departure (VERIFY timings).

198
00:14:59.833 --> 00:15:02.287
Journey 5: booking a flight on an OTA.

199
00:15:02.567 --> 00:15:06.983
Search: the user searches BLR, then DEL, 20 Dec, 1 adult.

200
00:15:07.263 --> 00:15:14.217
Cache first: the flight search service checks its fare cache for recent results for this route-date (§6.8).

201
00:15:14.497 --> 00:15:17.591
If the cache is fresh enough, results return in 1 s.

202
00:15:17.871 --> 00:15:19.620
Otherwise it queries suppliers:

203
00:15:19.900 --> 00:15:25.297
GDS (Amadeus, Sabre, Travelport) for full-service and some low-cost carriers;.

204
00:15:25.577 --> 00:15:31.380
NDC APIs directly from airlines for richer offers (fare families, ancillaries);.

205
00:15:32.730 --> 00:15:34.906
low-cost carrier direct APIs.

206
00:15:35.186 --> 00:15:38.535
Results are merged, deduplicated and sorted, and cached.

207
00:15:38.815 --> 00:15:41.759
Look-to-book: hundreds of searches per booking are normal.

208
00:15:42.039 --> 00:15:46.562
Supplier calls are expensive and rate-limited, which is why caching matters.

209
00:15:46.842 --> 00:15:51.045
Stale cache means price changes at booking, so price-check before payment.

210
00:15:51.325 --> 00:15:55.527
Offer selection and price check: the user picks a flight and fare family.

211
00:15:55.807 --> 00:15:58.197
The OTA re-prices with the supplier.

212
00:15:58.477 --> 00:16:01.549
If the fare changed, the user is told before paying.

213
00:16:01.829 --> 00:16:02.575
Booking:

214
00:16:03.925 --> 00:16:20.011
create a PNR with the airline or GDS (passengers, contact, segments);. take payment;. issue the ticket (e-ticket number) through the GDS or airline;. if ticketing fails after payment, retry, then auto-refund with a clear message.

215
00:16:20.291 --> 00:16:24.173
Post-booking: web check-in links, seat selection and ancillaries.

216
00:16:24.453 --> 00:16:34.309
Schedule changes pushed by airlines (queues in the GDS, or NDC notifications) are processed, and customers informed and offered options.

217
00:16:35.659 --> 00:16:46.283
Settlement: with airlines through industry settlement systems (BSP for IATA agents) or direct arrangements; OTA commission or markup accounting (VERIFY).

218
00:16:47.633 --> 00:16:49.511
Journey 6: booking a bus seat.

219
00:16:49.791 --> 00:16:53.247
Search: the user searches Bengaluru, then Chennai, tonight.

220
00:16:53.527 --> 00:17:02.060
Bus inventory comes from operators' reservation systems through APIs, or from operators using the aggregator's inventory software.

221
00:17:02.340 --> 00:17:10.617
Seat map: a seat layout (sleeper or seater, lower or upper) shows availability, from the operator in real time or with short caching.

222
00:17:10.897 --> 00:17:16.380
Booking: a seat block (hold) with the operator for a few minutes, then payment, then confirmation.

223
00:17:16.660 --> 00:17:18.111
Timeouts release the block.

224
00:17:18.391 --> 00:17:27.948
Journey day: boarding point details, live bus tracking (GPS from the operator; Ch 47), and cancellation per the operator's policy.

225
00:17:29.298 --> 00:17:30.258
Part 5.

226
00:17:30.538 --> 00:17:32.074
Core Subsystems in Depth.

227
00:17:33.324 --> 00:17:34.945
The train inventory model.

228
00:17:35.225 --> 00:17:39.833
Route segments: a train's route is a sequence of stops S0, S1,...

229
00:17:40.113 --> 00:17:40.924
Sn.

230
00:17:41.204 --> 00:17:45.471
A journey from Si to Sj occupies segments i... jminus 1.

231
00:17:45.751 --> 00:17:51.297
Berths: each coach has berths (lower, middle, upper, side lower, side upper per class layout).

232
00:17:51.577 --> 00:17:58.233
Each berth's occupancy is a bitset of length n: bit k set means the berth is occupied on segment k.

233
00:17:58.513 --> 00:18:04.721
A journey fits a berth if the AND of the berth's bitset and the journey mask is zero (§6.1).

234
00:18:06.071 --> 00:18:17.186
Quotas: berths are assigned to quota pools (general, Tatkal, ladies, senior or lower berth, defence, pooled quotas for intermediate stations; VERIFY names and rules).

235
00:18:17.466 --> 00:18:24.079
Pools can have remote locations and roadside station quotas, so intermediate stations get fair shares.

236
00:18:24.359 --> 00:18:32.637
Quotas may be released to other pools at defined times (for example, unused quota released to general before charting; VERIFY).

237
00:18:32.917 --> 00:18:36.479
Waitlists: per quota per train-date, ordered by booking time.

238
00:18:36.759 --> 00:18:41.346
RAC: side lower berths are shared by two passengers until a berth frees up.

239
00:18:42.696 --> 00:18:52.659
State size: a train with 22 coaches times 72 berths about 1,600 berths times a bitset of ~30 segments is tiny.

240
00:18:52.939 --> 00:19:00.875
All inventory for one train-date fits in memory in the booking worker, which is why a single-threaded worker per partition is fast.

241
00:19:02.225 --> 00:19:03.803
Booking engine partitions.

242
00:19:04.083 --> 00:19:18.334
One owner per (train, date): the worker loads the inventory into memory;. it processes booking and cancellation commands serially from its queue;. it writes each result to a durable log (and the database) before acknowledging.

243
00:19:18.614 --> 00:19:26.998
This is the single-writer model of Ch 55: no locks, no double allocation, and deterministic replay after a crash.

244
00:19:28.348 --> 00:19:37.457
Partition count: there are a lot of trains times dates (advance reservation period of ~60 days or more; VERIFY), but most are cold.

245
00:19:37.737 --> 00:19:39.529
Active hot partitions are few.

246
00:19:39.809 --> 00:19:44.417
Partitions are assigned to workers by consistent hashing, and moved on failure.

247
00:19:44.697 --> 00:19:48.623
Throughput per partition: thousands of commands per second in memory.

248
00:19:48.903 --> 00:19:52.721
During Tatkal, a popular train-date partition is the hot spot.

249
00:19:53.001 --> 00:19:58.057
The admission controller queues requests for it rather than letting them retry and pile up.

250
00:19:59.407 --> 00:20:00.730
Availability cache.

251
00:20:01.010 --> 00:20:15.282
Per (train, date, class, quota, from, to): there are too many combinations to precompute every pair for long routes, so the cache stores per-segment free counts per quota and computes the minimum over the journey's segments on request.

252
00:20:15.562 --> 00:20:18.634
It also caches recent answers for popular pairs.

253
00:20:18.914 --> 00:20:25.655
Updates: from inventory events (booking, cancellation, quota release), pushed to cache nodes within seconds.

254
00:20:25.935 --> 00:20:28.282
During peaks: the cache may lag the truth.

255
00:20:28.562 --> 00:20:32.274
The booking result is final, and the UI communicates it clearly.

256
00:20:33.624 --> 00:20:35.651
Waitlist, RAC and charting.

257
00:20:35.931 --> 00:20:40.432
Promotion runs inside the train-date partition, after each cancellation.

258
00:20:40.712 --> 00:20:44.467
It re-checks segment fit for each waitlisted passenger in order.

259
00:20:44.747 --> 00:20:50.037
A long-distance cancellation may confirm several short-distance waitlisted passengers.

260
00:20:50.317 --> 00:20:52.173
A short one may confirm nobody.

261
00:20:52.453 --> 00:20:56.379
Charting: a scheduled command to each partition at the charting time;.

262
00:20:57.729 --> 00:21:11.339
it freezes allocations, generates the reservation chart (for coaches and the ticket examiner's handheld device), cancels unconfirmed waitlisted e-tickets with refunds, and publishes vacant berths for current booking.

263
00:21:11.619 --> 00:21:25.273
Handheld devices for ticket examiners can update berth occupancy on board (no-shows, on-board allocation of vacant berths to RAC passengers), synchronised when connectivity allows (VERIFY deployment).

264
00:21:26.623 --> 00:21:28.393
Payments and refunds at peak.

265
00:21:28.673 --> 00:21:31.724
Idempotent payment callbacks keyed by booking reference.

266
00:21:32.004 --> 00:21:34.095
Hold timers in the booking partition.

267
00:21:34.375 --> 00:21:38.513
A late payment success after hold expiry, then try to re-allocate.

268
00:21:38.793 --> 00:21:42.100
If that's impossible, auto-refund within a defined time.

269
00:21:42.380 --> 00:21:49.079
Refund engine: rules by ticket status, class, time before departure and Tatkal status (VERIFY).

270
00:21:49.359 --> 00:21:51.279
Refunds go to the source instrument.

271
00:21:51.559 --> 00:21:54.311
Reconciliation and customer notification.

272
00:21:55.661 --> 00:21:57.581
OTA: supplier connectivity.

273
00:21:57.861 --> 00:22:04.581
GDS: a global distribution system aggregates many airlines' schedules, availability and fares.

274
00:22:04.861 --> 00:22:09.127
It is accessed through APIs (historically cryptic terminal commands).

275
00:22:09.407 --> 00:22:14.229
There is a cost per transaction and per segment booked (VERIFY commercial models).

276
00:22:14.509 --> 00:22:15.490
NDC:

277
00:22:15.770 --> 00:22:25.925
IATA's New Distribution Capability, XML or JSON APIs direct from airlines, with richer content (bundles, ancillaries, personalised offers).

278
00:22:26.205 --> 00:22:29.255
Low-cost carrier APIs: direct connections.

279
00:22:30.605 --> 00:22:38.115
Normalisation: all responses map to an internal offer model (segments, fares, rules, baggage, refundability).

280
00:22:38.395 --> 00:22:41.552
Supplier-specific quirks are isolated in connectors.

281
00:22:41.832 --> 00:22:47.549
Resilience: timeouts per supplier; partial results are fine (some airlines didn't respond).

282
00:22:47.829 --> 00:22:49.877
Circuit breakers (Ch 18).

283
00:22:51.227 --> 00:22:52.166
Part 6.

284
00:22:52.446 --> 00:22:53.406
Algorithms.

285
00:22:54.656 --> 00:22:56.512
Segment inventory with bitsets.

286
00:22:56.792 --> 00:23:01.571
Journey mask: for journey (i, j), the mask has bits i... jminus 1 set.

287
00:23:01.851 --> 00:23:06.352
Fit check: a berth b fits if occupancyb AND mask == 0.

288
00:23:06.632 --> 00:23:08.829
Book it by occupancyb = mask.

289
00:23:09.109 --> 00:23:12.267
Cancel by clearing the mask bits (only this booking's).

290
00:23:12.547 --> 00:23:19.160
Free count per segment for availability: count berths with bit k clear per quota, maintained incrementally.

291
00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:25.413
Performance: a few thousand berths times one AND each is microseconds per request, in memory.

292
00:23:26.763 --> 00:23:28.555
Berth allocation heuristics.

293
00:23:28.835 --> 00:23:29.923
Goals, in order:

294
00:23:30.203 --> 00:23:31.547
Satisfy the journey.

295
00:23:31.827 --> 00:23:36.606
Honour preferences and priority rules (seniors, women, children; VERIFY).

296
00:23:36.886 --> 00:23:38.955
Keep a group in the same coach or bay.

297
00:23:39.235 --> 00:23:44.313
Minimise fragmentation, so the remaining capacity stays useful for future journeys.

298
00:23:45.663 --> 00:23:50.527
Fragmentation example: a berth free for segments 0 to 10 is valuable.

299
00:23:50.807 --> 00:23:58.465
Using it for a 4 to 6 journey leaves two short holes (0 to 4 and 6 to 10) that fewer future journeys can use.

300
00:23:58.745 --> 00:24:08.537
Best-fit heuristic: among fitting berths, prefer the one where the booked segments touch existing occupancy (the berth is already occupied just before or after).

301
00:24:08.817 --> 00:24:13.553
That fills gaps rather than creating new ones, like best-fit in memory allocation.

302
00:24:13.833 --> 00:24:21.727
Quota and pool rules: allocate from the journey's quota pool first, then from designated overflow pools per rules (VERIFY).

303
00:24:23.077 --> 00:24:29.925
Evaluation: simulate booking streams to compare heuristics on seats sold and berth-km utilisation.

304
00:24:30.205 --> 00:24:33.533
Railways' actual algorithms are not public (VERIFY).

305
00:24:34.883 --> 00:24:36.184
Waitlist promotion.

306
00:24:36.464 --> 00:24:43.504
The ordered list per quota: for each waitlisted passenger in order, try to fit their journey into the released capacity.

307
00:24:43.784 --> 00:24:52.829
A head-of-line rule decides whether a later passenger can confirm when the first can't (for example, because their segments don't fit), per rules (VERIFY).

308
00:24:53.109 --> 00:25:03.243
RAC passengers are promoted to confirmed berths when a full berth frees for their segment, and waitlisted passengers move into vacated RAC places.

309
00:25:04.593 --> 00:25:06.385
Waitlist confirmation prediction.

310
00:25:06.665 --> 00:25:07.603
The question:

311
00:25:07.883 --> 00:25:13.110
WL 42 on train X, travel in 10 days, 3AC, will it confirm?

312
00:25:13.390 --> 00:25:24.547
Features: train, class, quota, waitlist number, days to departure, day of week, season and festivals, historical cancellation curves for this train and class, current booking pace.

313
00:25:24.827 --> 00:25:30.630
Model: historical data on waitlist movement, then a probability of confirmation by charting.

314
00:25:30.910 --> 00:25:35.475
A gradient-boosted classifier or survival-style models of cancellations.

315
00:25:35.755 --> 00:25:38.827
Several travel apps offer such predictions (VERIFY).

316
00:25:40.177 --> 00:25:45.191
Use: helps users decide whether to book the waitlisted ticket or choose another train.

317
00:25:45.471 --> 00:25:46.900
Suggest alternatives.

318
00:25:48.250 --> 00:25:49.679
Anti-bot and fairness.

319
00:25:49.959 --> 00:26:03.570
Signals: request timing too fast for humans (forms filled in milliseconds);. scripted browser fingerprints, headless browsers;. many accounts per device or IP or payment instrument;. known automation tools;.

320
00:26:03.850 --> 00:26:05.386
CAPTCHA solving patterns.

321
00:26:05.666 --> 00:26:06.583
Controls:

322
00:26:07.933 --> 00:26:25.725
CAPTCHAs (with accessibility options);. device attestation in the mobile app;. per-user and per-instrument limits;. restrictions on agents during the opening minutes (VERIFY);. server-side queueing, so speed of retries doesn't help;. after-the-fact cancellation of tickets from detected bot accounts.

323
00:26:26.005 --> 00:26:27.221
Fairness principle:

324
00:26:27.501 --> 00:26:30.381
FIFO at the partition among admitted requests.

325
00:26:30.661 --> 00:26:35.717
The admission controller never lets fast retries jump the queue (Ch 46).

326
00:26:37.067 --> 00:26:38.945
Train search across a timetable.

327
00:26:39.225 --> 00:26:44.835
Direct trains: index trains by station, and for each station pair check the stop order.

328
00:26:45.115 --> 00:26:49.851
Connections (optional): a time-dependent graph (stations and departure events).

329
00:26:50.131 --> 00:26:56.937
Earliest-arrival search with CSA (Connection Scan Algorithm) or RAPTOR, with a minimum transfer time.

330
00:26:57.217 --> 00:27:01.889
Journey planners use these to find two-train options when direct ones are full.

331
00:27:03.239 --> 00:27:04.412
Dynamic fares.

332
00:27:04.692 --> 00:27:14.889
Flexi fares on some premium trains: the fare increases in steps as a percentage of berths are sold, up to a cap (VERIFY the current scheme and trains covered).

333
00:27:15.169 --> 00:27:22.423
Implementation: the fare engine reads sold percentage per train-date-class from inventory and applies the step function.

334
00:27:22.703 --> 00:27:24.772
The quoted fare is held with the booking.

335
00:27:25.052 --> 00:27:33.415
Airline pricing in OTAs: fares come from airlines' revenue management (booking classes opened and closed by demand forecasts).

336
00:27:33.695 --> 00:27:36.255
The OTA shows them; it doesn't set them.

337
00:27:37.605 --> 00:27:38.949
Fare cache for flights.

338
00:27:39.229 --> 00:27:40.039
Keys:

339
00:27:40.319 --> 00:27:45.738
(origin, destination, date, passenger mix, cabin), plus result sets from suppliers.

340
00:27:46.018 --> 00:27:53.314
TTL by volatility: short for near-term departures, whose fares change fast, and longer for far-future dates.

341
00:27:53.594 --> 00:27:57.818
Refresh: popular routes are pre-fetched in the background from search logs.

342
00:27:58.098 --> 00:28:01.085
Refresh on booking attempts that show price changes.

343
00:28:01.365 --> 00:28:06.655
Measure: price-change rate at price-check (the cache's lie rate) versus supplier cost.

344
00:28:06.935 --> 00:28:10.775
Tune TTLs per route and date band (Ch 07).

345
00:28:12.125 --> 00:28:13.405
Demand forecasting.

346
00:28:13.685 --> 00:28:21.408
For railways: demand per train, class and date from historical bookings and waitlists, holidays and festivals, and events.

347
00:28:21.688 --> 00:28:26.595
It is used for special trains, extra coaches, and quota allocation decisions.

348
00:28:26.875 --> 00:28:33.253
For OTAs: search volume forecasts for capacity planning, and pre-fetching fare caches for trending routes.

