Kubernetes for stateless services; dedicated nodes for booking workers.

464
00:32:36.618 --> 00:32:37.429
Notes:

465
00:32:37.709 --> 00:32:39.117
Predictable latency.

466
00:32:40.467 --> 00:32:41.363
Part 9.

467
00:32:41.643 --> 00:32:43.840
Security, Fairness and Compliance.

468
00:32:45.090 --> 00:32:53.730
Identity: verified accounts (mobile or email, possibly Aadhaar-based verification for higher booking limits; VERIFY current rules).

469
00:32:54.010 --> 00:32:56.421
ID proof is required during travel.

470
00:32:56.701 --> 00:33:01.629
Agents: registered agents with separate rules and timing restrictions (VERIFY).

471
00:33:01.909 --> 00:33:06.943
Payment security: tokenised cards and gateway compliance (Ch 45).

472
00:33:07.223 --> 00:33:14.711
Privacy: passenger data (names, ages, IDs) protected, with limited retention (DPDP Act; VERIFY).

473
00:33:16.061 --> 00:33:28.072
Fairness and transparency: published rules for quotas, refunds and waitlists, consistent enforcement, and anti-bot measures explained at a high level without revealing detection details.

474
00:33:29.422 --> 00:33:30.275
Part 10.

475
00:33:30.555 --> 00:33:32.475
Reliability and Operations.

476
00:33:33.725 --> 00:33:50.131
Tatkal readiness: daily pre-scaling before the opening times;. waiting room configured;. search degraded mode ready;. payment gateways notified of peak;. dashboards per partition (queue depth, processing time), per gateway (success rate), and for bot blocks.

477
00:33:51.481 --> 00:33:56.259
Partition failover: each booking worker has a standby replaying the same log.

478
00:33:56.539 --> 00:34:00.230
Failover within seconds, while queued requests wait, not fail.

479
00:34:00.510 --> 00:34:10.537
Graceful degradation: order of shedding: recommendations and non-essential APIs;. live availability refresh (serve cached);. search for far-future dates.

480
00:34:10.817 --> 00:34:12.737
Protect booking and payment callbacks.

481
00:34:14.087 --> 00:34:17.671
Charting jobs are scheduled per train and must finish on time.

482
00:34:17.951 --> 00:34:19.316
Monitor late charts.

483
00:34:19.596 --> 00:34:23.201
OTA: supplier outages are handled by partial results.

484
00:34:23.481 --> 00:34:25.465
GDS connectivity failover.

485
00:34:25.745 --> 00:34:33.340
Airline disruption events (weather) cause mass schedule changes, processed from queues with customer communication at scale.

486
00:34:34.690 --> 00:34:35.693
Part 11.

487
00:34:35.973 --> 00:34:37.018
Key Trade-offs.

488
00:34:38.268 --> 00:34:39.612
Here is a quick table:

489
00:34:39.892 --> 00:34:42.409
Decision, and Chosen, and Accepted cost.

490
00:34:42.689 --> 00:34:44.119
Inventory ownership.

491
00:34:44.399 --> 00:34:45.231
Chosen:

492
00:34:45.511 --> 00:34:47.175
Single writer per train-date.

493
00:34:47.455 --> 00:34:48.628
Accepted cost:

494
00:34:48.908 --> 00:34:51.063
Hot partitions during Tatkal must queue.

495
00:34:51.343 --> 00:34:52.345
Allocation.

496
00:34:52.625 --> 00:34:53.457
Chosen:

497
00:34:53.737 --> 00:34:55.572
Heuristic best-fit per segment.

498
00:34:55.852 --> 00:34:57.025
Accepted cost:

499
00:34:57.305 --> 00:34:58.692
Not globally optimal.

500
00:34:58.972 --> 00:35:00.039
Availability.

501
00:35:00.319 --> 00:35:01.151
Chosen:

502
00:35:01.431 --> 00:35:03.287
Cached, eventually consistent.

503
00:35:03.567 --> 00:35:04.740
Accepted cost:

504
00:35:05.020 --> 00:35:06.919
Users may see seats that are gone.

505
00:35:07.199 --> 00:35:08.265
Hold then pay.

506
00:35:08.545 --> 00:35:09.377
Chosen:

507
00:35:09.657 --> 00:35:10.767
Bounded holds.

508
00:35:11.047 --> 00:35:12.220
Accepted cost:

509
00:35:12.500 --> 00:35:15.060
Seats locked briefly by users who don't pay.

510
00:35:15.340 --> 00:35:16.343
Anti-bot.

511
00:35:16.623 --> 00:35:17.455
Chosen:

512
00:35:17.735 --> 00:35:19.463
Layered controls + FIFO.

513
00:35:19.743 --> 00:35:20.916
Accepted cost:

514
00:35:21.196 --> 00:35:23.137
Some friction for genuine users.

515
00:35:23.417 --> 00:35:24.548
OTA caching.

516
00:35:24.828 --> 00:35:25.660
Chosen:

517
00:35:25.940 --> 00:35:28.500
TTL by volatility + price check.

518
00:35:28.780 --> 00:35:29.953
Accepted cost:

519
00:35:30.233 --> 00:35:31.727
Price changes at booking.

520
00:35:33.077 --> 00:35:34.015
Part 12.

521
00:35:34.295 --> 00:35:35.639
Interview Q and A.

522
00:35:36.889 --> 00:35:37.721
Beginner.

523
00:35:38.001 --> 00:35:39.025
Question 1.

524
00:35:39.305 --> 00:35:41.972
What makes train ticketing harder than flight ticketing?

525
00:35:42.252 --> 00:35:43.617
Thirty-second answer:

526
00:35:43.897 --> 00:35:57.423
A train serves many overlapping journeys along its route, so each berth's occupancy must be tracked per segment; plus quotas, RAC, waitlists that confirm on cancellations, charting, and demand spikes like Tatkal.

527
00:35:58.773 --> 00:35:59.775
Question 2.

528
00:36:00.055 --> 00:36:02.061
What happens when you book a train ticket?

529
00:36:02.341 --> 00:36:03.706
Thirty-second answer:

530
00:36:03.986 --> 00:36:20.349
Search and availability from a cache; submit passengers; the booking engine for that train-date allocates berths (or RAC or waitlist) and holds them; you pay; on success a PNR is created and the e-ticket sent; on failure the hold is released.

531
00:36:21.699 --> 00:36:22.744
Question 3.

532
00:36:23.024 --> 00:36:24.219
What is a PNR?

533
00:36:24.499 --> 00:36:25.864
Thirty-second answer:

534
00:36:26.144 --> 00:36:37.579
The passenger name record, a booking reference holding the train, date, class, passengers, their berth or waitlist status, fare and payment details; used to check status and on board.

535
00:36:38.929 --> 00:36:39.995
Question 4.

536
00:36:40.275 --> 00:36:42.771
Why cache availability separately from bookings?

537
00:36:43.051 --> 00:36:44.417
Thirty-second answer:

538
00:36:44.697 --> 00:36:56.899
Availability is queried far more often than bookings are made; serving it from a cache updated by inventory events protects the booking engine, at the cost of occasionally showing seats that were just sold.

539
00:36:58.249 --> 00:36:59.252
Intermediate.

540
00:36:59.532 --> 00:37:00.641
Question 5.

541
00:37:00.921 --> 00:37:03.972
How do you represent and allocate berths along a route?

542
00:37:04.252 --> 00:37:05.617
Thirty-second answer:

543
00:37:05.897 --> 00:37:22.324
Each berth has a bitset over route segments; a journey is a mask of its segments; a berth fits if AND is zero; book by OR, cancel by clearing; choose among fitting berths with best-fit heuristics that reduce fragmentation and honour preferences and quotas.

544
00:37:23.674 --> 00:37:24.783
Question 6.

545
00:37:25.063 --> 00:37:27.773
How do waitlist and RAC promotions work?

546
00:37:28.053 --> 00:37:29.418
Thirty-second answer:

547
00:37:29.698 --> 00:37:49.645
On cancellation, the released berth-segments are offered to RAC passengers first (moving them to full berths), then waitlisted passengers move into RAC or confirmed places in order, per quota and segment fit, and passengers are notified; at charting unconfirmed e-tickets are cancelled with refunds.

548
00:37:50.995 --> 00:37:52.083
Question 7.

549
00:37:52.363 --> 00:37:54.688
How do you prevent double booking of a berth?

550
00:37:54.968 --> 00:37:56.333
Thirty-second answer:

551
00:37:56.613 --> 00:38:08.091
Partition inventory by train and date with a single writer processing commands serially in memory, journaling each allocation before acknowledging; different train-dates proceed in parallel.

552
00:38:09.441 --> 00:38:10.465
Question 8.

553
00:38:10.745 --> 00:38:13.945
How does an OTA search flights across airlines quickly?

554
00:38:14.225 --> 00:38:15.590
Thirty-second answer:

555
00:38:15.870 --> 00:38:33.577
Check a fare cache keyed by route, date and passengers with TTLs based on volatility; on miss, query GDS, NDC and low-cost carrier APIs in parallel with timeouts, normalise into one offer model, merge, sort and cache, then price-check before booking.

556
00:38:34.927 --> 00:38:35.993
Question 9.

557
00:38:36.273 --> 00:38:39.751
What happens if payment succeeds but the ticket isn't issued?

558
00:38:40.031 --> 00:38:41.396
Thirty-second answer:

559
00:38:41.676 --> 00:38:55.415
Retry issuance or allocation with idempotent keys; if still impossible (hold expired, seats gone, ticketing failed), auto-refund within a defined time and notify the user, never keep money without a ticket.

560
00:38:56.765 --> 00:38:57.533
Senior.

561
00:38:57.813 --> 00:38:58.858
Question 10.

562
00:38:59.138 --> 00:39:01.229
Design the system for the Tatkal minute.

563
00:39:01.509 --> 00:39:02.874
Thirty-second answer:

564
00:39:03.154 --> 00:39:25.426
Pre-scale; CDN for static content; a waiting room and server-synced countdown; anti-bot and per-user limits; an admission controller routing requests FIFO into single-writer train-date partitions; short holds with payment spread across gateways; cached availability and degraded search; fast not available answers; post-hoc bot cancellations.

565
00:39:26.776 --> 00:39:27.907
Question 11.

566
00:39:28.187 --> 00:39:30.427
How would you predict waitlist confirmation?

567
00:39:30.707 --> 00:39:32.072
Thirty-second answer:

568
00:39:32.352 --> 00:39:46.624
Train a model on historical waitlist movement with features like train, class, quota, WL number, days to departure, seasonality and current booking pace; output a probability by charting; show it with alternatives.

569
00:39:47.974 --> 00:39:49.062
Question 12.

570
00:39:49.342 --> 00:39:51.518
How do you detect bots in ticket booking?

571
00:39:51.798 --> 00:39:53.163
Thirty-second answer:

572
00:39:53.443 --> 00:40:12.494
Timing and behaviour signals, browser fingerprints and headless detection, device attestation in apps, account or device or payment clustering, CAPTCHA patterns, combined into risk scores that trigger challenges, limits, blocks and later cancellations, with server-side FIFO so speed can't win.

573
00:40:13.844 --> 00:40:15.124
Question 13.

574
00:40:15.404 --> 00:40:18.625
How would you design the OTA's booking and ticketing flow?

575
00:40:18.905 --> 00:40:20.271
Thirty-second answer:

576
00:40:20.551 --> 00:40:36.423
Price-check, create a PNR with the supplier, take payment, issue the ticket, and handle failures with retries and refunds; process airline schedule changes from queues or notifications; reconcile with airline settlement and supplier invoices.

577
00:40:37.773 --> 00:40:38.967
Question 14.

578
00:40:39.247 --> 00:40:42.063
How do dynamic fares on trains work in the system?

579
00:40:42.343 --> 00:40:43.709
Thirty-second answer:

580
00:40:43.989 --> 00:40:58.005
The fare engine reads sold percentage per train-date-class from inventory and applies the scheme's step function up to a cap; the quoted fare is fixed with the booking; rules are configurable per train (VERIFY scheme details).

581
00:40:59.355 --> 00:41:00.251
Architect.

582
00:41:00.531 --> 00:41:01.725
Question 15.

583
00:41:02.005 --> 00:41:04.843
Design a national railway reservation system.

584
00:41:05.123 --> 00:41:06.488
Thirty-second answer:

585
00:41:06.768 --> 00:41:20.144
Timetable and train master; in-memory inventory per train-date with segment bitsets and quotas, owned by single-writer partitions with journals and standbys; booking with holds and payment orchestration; waitlist,

586
00:41:20.424 --> 00:41:33.181
RAC and charting jobs; cached availability; cancellation and refund rules; admission control and anti-bot for Tatkal; Kafka to a warehouse for forecasting, waitlist prediction and fraud.

587
00:41:34.531 --> 00:41:35.726
Question 16.

588
00:41:36.006 --> 00:41:39.505
How would you evaluate a new berth allocation algorithm safely?

589
00:41:39.785 --> 00:41:41.150
Thirty-second answer:

590
00:41:41.430 --> 00:41:57.707
Replay historical booking streams through a simulator with the current and new heuristics, compare berth-km utilisation, confirmed tickets and preference satisfaction, test edge cases (groups, quotas), then pilot on a few trains with monitoring before rollout.

591
00:41:59.057 --> 00:42:00.401
Question 17.

592
00:42:00.681 --> 00:42:03.732
How would you modernise a legacy reservation system?

593
00:42:04.012 --> 00:42:05.377
Thirty-second answer:

594
00:42:05.657 --> 00:42:22.703
Put APIs and an event stream around the legacy core, move read paths (search, availability, PNR status) out first, then migrate booking partitions train by train with dual-run comparison of allocations, keeping the journal as the reconciliation point.

595
00:42:24.053 --> 00:42:25.226
Question 18.

596
00:42:25.506 --> 00:42:29.517
How do you balance fairness and efficiency in scarce inventory systems?

597
00:42:29.797 --> 00:42:31.162
Thirty-second answer:

598
00:42:31.442 --> 00:42:50.514
Define fairness (FIFO among admitted, limits per person), enforce it server-side (queues, not races), defend against bots, keep quotas and published rules, measure outcomes (success rates by segment of users), and accept that efficiency optimisations must not undermine perceived fairness.

599
00:42:51.864 --> 00:42:53.101
Question 19.

600
00:42:53.381 --> 00:42:56.816
What does the look-to-book ratio mean for OTA architecture?

601
00:42:57.096 --> 00:42:58.461
Thirty-second answer:

602
00:42:58.741 --> 00:43:15.488
Searches vastly outnumber bookings and suppliers charge or throttle per call, so the architecture centres on caching, pre-fetching and cost-aware supplier routing, with price checks to manage staleness, measured by cache hit rate, lie rate and supplier cost per booking.

603
00:43:16.838 --> 00:43:17.947
Question 20.

604
00:43:18.227 --> 00:43:21.555
Compare railway reservation with an e-commerce flash sale.

605
00:43:21.835 --> 00:43:23.201
Thirty-second answer:

606
00:43:23.481 --> 00:43:42.318
Both face demand far above supply, need fair admission, holds and payment orchestration; railways add segment-based inventory, quotas, waitlists that confirm later, charting deadlines and regulatory rules, so inventory logic is richer, while admission and payment patterns are shared.

607
00:43:43.668 --> 00:43:44.713
Part 13.

608
00:43:44.993 --> 00:43:45.761
Summary.

609
00:43:47.011 --> 00:43:49.742
Train inventory is per berth per route segment.

610
00:43:50.022 --> 00:43:53.393
Bitsets make fit checks and allocations trivial in memory.

611
00:43:53.673 --> 00:43:58.878
Single-writer partitions per train-date guarantee no double booking and simple recovery.

612
00:43:59.158 --> 00:44:01.035
Cached availability protects them.

613
00:44:01.315 --> 00:44:06.627
Waitlists, RAC and charting are rule-driven processes inside the partition.

614
00:44:06.907 --> 00:44:08.742
Predictions help users decide.

615
00:44:09.022 --> 00:44:16.595
The Tatkal minute is won with admission control, anti-bot measures, FIFO queues, bounded holds and payment orchestration.

616
00:44:17.945 --> 00:44:23.279
OTAs live on caching and supplier normalisation because of the look-to-book ratio.

617
00:44:23.559 --> 00:44:26.801
Price checks and reliable ticketing protect customers.

618
00:44:28.151 --> 00:44:29.389
